const CARDS_OPHTHO_RED_EYE = [
{ q: "Name the two types of conjunctiva and their locations.", a: "<b>Bulbar conjunctiva</b> — covers the anterior sclera (white of the eye)<br><b>Palpebral (tarsal) conjunctiva</b> — lines the inner surface of the eyelids<br>They are continuous at the <b>fornix</b> (conjunctival cul-de-sac)." },
{ q: "List the 5 layers of the cornea from anterior to posterior.", a: "1. <b>Epithelium</b> (stratified squamous, rapid regeneration)<br>2. <b>Bowman's layer</b> (acellular collagen, does NOT regenerate)<br>3. <b>Stroma</b> (90% of corneal thickness, type I collagen lamellae)<br>4. <b>Descemet's membrane</b> (basement membrane of endothelium, regenerates)<br>5. <b>Endothelium</b> (single layer, maintains corneal dehydration via Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase pump, does NOT regenerate)" },
{ q: "What are the three structures of the uveal tract?", a: "<b>Iris</b> — controls pupil size (sphincter = parasympathetic, dilator = sympathetic)<br><b>Ciliary body</b> — produces aqueous humor, controls accommodation<br><b>Choroid</b> — vascular layer that supplies outer retina (RPE & photoreceptors)" },
{ q: "What is the limbus and why is it clinically important?", a: "The <b>limbus</b> is the junction between the cornea and sclera.<br>It contains <b>limbal stem cells</b> that regenerate corneal epithelium.<br>It also houses the <b>trabecular meshwork</b> and <b>Schlemm's canal</b> for aqueous drainage.<br><i>Limbal stem cell deficiency → corneal opacification and vascularization.</i>" },
{ q: "What is the anterior chamber and what does it contain?", a: "The <b>anterior chamber</b> is the space between the cornea (anterior) and the iris/pupil (posterior).<br>It is filled with <b>aqueous humor</b> produced by the ciliary body.<br>The <b>iridocorneal angle</b> at its periphery contains the trabecular meshwork for aqueous outflow." },
{ q: "What is a ciliary flush (circumcorneal/limbal flush) and what does it indicate?", a: "A <b>ciliary flush</b> is a ring of deep, violaceous injection surrounding the limbus.<br>It indicates <b>deep ocular inflammation</b>:<br>• <b>Anterior uveitis</b><br>• <b>Keratitis</b><br>• <b>Acute angle-closure glaucoma</b><br>It is supplied by the <b>anterior ciliary arteries</b> (deep episcleral vessels)." },
{ q: "Differentiate conjunctival injection from ciliary flush.", a: "<b>Conjunctival injection</b>: superficial, bright red, <i>most prominent in fornices</i>, fades toward limbus, blanches with phenylephrine — seen in <b>conjunctivitis</b><br><b>Ciliary flush</b>: deep, violaceous, <i>most prominent at limbus</i>, does NOT blanch with topical phenylephrine — seen in <b>uveitis, keratitis, acute glaucoma</b>" },
{ q: "Give a systematic approach to the red eye differential using 5 key features.", a: "Assess: <b>Pain, Vision, Pupil, Discharge, Injection pattern</b><br>• <b>Conjunctivitis</b>: no/mild pain, normal vision, normal pupil, discharge present, diffuse injection<br>• <b>Keratitis</b>: pain, ↓ vision, normal pupil, watery/purulent, ciliary flush<br>• <b>Anterior uveitis</b>: pain + photophobia, ↓ vision, miotic pupil, no discharge, ciliary flush<br>• <b>Acute glaucoma</b>: severe pain + N/V, ↓↓ vision, fixed mid-dilated pupil, no discharge, diffuse + ciliary flush<br>• <b>Scleritis</b>: severe boring pain, ↓ vision possible, normal pupil, no discharge, deep injection" },
{ q: "Which red eye conditions cause vision loss?", a: "<b>Vision-threatening</b>:<br>• Keratitis (corneal ulcer)<br>• Anterior uveitis<br>• Acute angle-closure glaucoma<br>• Scleritis<br>• Endophthalmitis<br><b>Vision-sparing</b>:<br>• Conjunctivitis<br>• Episcleritis<br>• Subconjunctival hemorrhage" },
{ q: "What pupil abnormalities are seen in red eye conditions?", a: "<b>Miotic (small) pupil</b> → anterior uveitis (reflex spasm of sphincter from inflammation)<br><b>Mid-dilated, fixed pupil</b> → acute angle-closure glaucoma<br><b>Normal pupil</b> → conjunctivitis, episcleritis, scleritis, subconjunctival hemorrhage" },
{ q: "What are the most common organisms causing bacterial conjunctivitis?", a: "<b>Adults</b>:<br>• <b>Staphylococcus aureus</b> (most common in adults)<br>• Streptococcus pneumoniae<br>• Moraxella catarrhalis<br><b>Children</b>:<br>• <b>Haemophilus influenzae</b> (most common in children, can cause otitis media-conjunctivitis syndrome)<br>• Streptococcus pneumoniae" },
{ q: "Describe the clinical features of bacterial conjunctivitis.", a: "<b>Mucopurulent discharge</b> (yellow-green, crusting of lids in morning)<br>Conjunctival injection (diffuse, more in fornices)<br><b>Papillary reaction</b> on tarsal conjunctiva<br>Usually <b>unilateral initially</b>, spreads to other eye in 1–2 days<br>No preauricular lymphadenopathy (unlike viral)<br>Usually <b>self-limiting</b> in 5–7 days" },
{ q: "What is the treatment for routine bacterial conjunctivitis?", a: "Often <b>self-limiting</b>, but topical antibiotics shorten course:<br>• <b>Erythromycin 0.5% ointment</b> — first-line, especially in children<br>• <b>Fluoroquinolone drops</b> (moxifloxacin, ofloxacin)<br>• Trimethoprim-polymyxin B drops<br>Apply QID for 5–7 days.<br><b>Hand hygiene</b> to prevent spread." },
{ q: "What defines hyperacute bacterial conjunctivitis and what is the most common causative organism?", a: "<b>Hyperacute conjunctivitis</b>: onset within 12–24 hours with <b>copious purulent discharge</b> that reaccumulates rapidly after wiping.<br>Most common cause: <b>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</b><br>It is an <b>ophthalmologic emergency</b> due to risk of <b>corneal perforation</b>." },
{ q: "How is gonococcal conjunctivitis treated?", a: "<b>Systemic</b>: <b>Ceftriaxone 1g IM × 1 dose</b> (plus treat for concurrent chlamydia with azithromycin 1g PO)<br><b>Topical</b>: Erythromycin ointment or fluoroquinolone drops<br><b>Saline irrigation</b> of discharge<br><b>Urgent ophthalmology referral</b><br><i>Corneal perforation can occur within 24–48 hours if untreated!</i>" },
{ q: "Why is gonococcal conjunctivitis considered an ophthalmologic emergency?", a: "<b>N. gonorrhoeae</b> can penetrate <b>intact corneal epithelium</b> (unlike most bacteria), leading to:<br>• Rapid <b>corneal ulceration</b><br>• <b>Corneal perforation</b><br>• <b>Endophthalmitis</b><br>• Permanent <b>vision loss</b><br>Requires immediate treatment with systemic ceftriaxone." },
{ q: "What is the most common cause of viral conjunctivitis?", a: "<b>Adenovirus</b> (serotypes 3, 4, 7, 8, 19, 37)<br>It is the <b>most common overall cause of conjunctivitis</b>.<br>Highly contagious — spreads via direct contact, fomites, contaminated swimming pools.<br>Virus can survive on surfaces for weeks." },
{ q: "What are the clinical features of adenoviral conjunctivitis?", a: "• <b>Watery (serous) discharge</b><br>• <b>Preauricular lymphadenopathy</b> (hallmark of viral)<br>• <b>Follicular reaction</b> on palpebral conjunctiva<br>• Often preceded by URI or pharyngitis<br>• Bilateral (second eye affected 1–2 days later, usually milder)<br>• <b>Subconjunctival hemorrhage</b> may occur<br>Self-limiting in 1–3 weeks." },
{ q: "What is epidemic keratoconjunctivitis (EKC) and what complication can follow?", a: "<b>EKC</b> is a severe form of adenoviral conjunctivitis (serotypes 8, 19, 37).<br>Features: intense tearing, pseudomembranes, keratitis<br>Late complication: <b>subepithelial infiltrates (SEIs)</b> — focal immune deposits in corneal stroma appearing 1–3 weeks after infection.<br>SEIs can cause <b>glare and decreased vision</b> lasting months to years.<br>Treatment of SEIs: topical steroids if vision-impairing." },
{ q: "How is viral conjunctivitis managed?", a: "<b>Supportive care</b> — self-limiting in 1–3 weeks:<br>• Cold compresses<br>• Artificial tears<br>• Hand hygiene (highly contagious for 10–14 days)<br>• Avoid sharing towels/pillows<br><b>No antibiotics</b> (ineffective against viruses)<br>Pseudomembranes → peel with forceps, consider topical steroids" },
{ q: "How do you differentiate follicles from papillae on the conjunctiva?", a: "<b>Follicles</b>: small, pale, avascular lymphoid aggregates with vessels coursing <i>around</i> them<br>→ Seen in: <b>viral and chlamydial</b> conjunctivitis<br><br><b>Papillae</b>: red, vascular mounds with a central vessel core<br>→ Seen in: <b>bacterial and allergic</b> conjunctivitis<br>Giant papillae (>1 mm) → vernal keratoconjunctivitis, contact lens-related GPC" },
{ q: "What is the hallmark symptom of allergic conjunctivitis?", a: "<b>Itching</b> — the hallmark symptom (pruritis)<br>Other features: <b>bilateral</b>, chemosis (conjunctival edema), watery discharge with stringy/mucoid component, <b>papillary reaction</b>, lid edema<br>History of atopy (allergic rhinitis, asthma, eczema) is common." },
{ q: "What is found on conjunctival scraping in allergic conjunctivitis?", a: "<b>Eosinophils</b> on conjunctival cytology<br>This distinguishes allergic from infectious causes.<br>Mast cell degranulation → release of histamine, tryptase, prostaglandins → itching and chemosis." },
{ q: "What is the treatment for allergic conjunctivitis?", a: "<b>First-line</b>: <b>Dual-action antihistamine/mast cell stabilizers</b> — <b>olopatadine</b> (Patanol), ketotifen<br><b>Mast cell stabilizers</b>: cromolyn sodium, nedocromil (prophylactic, take 2 weeks to work)<br><b>Antihistamine drops</b>: azelastine, emedastine<br><b>Supportive</b>: cold compresses, artificial tears, avoid rubbing eyes<br><b>Oral antihistamines</b> if severe systemic symptoms<br>Topical steroids for severe flares only (short course, monitor IOP)" },
{ q: "What is vernal keratoconjunctivitis and how does it differ from seasonal allergic conjunctivitis?", a: "<b>Vernal keratoconjunctivitis</b>: chronic, bilateral, affects <b>young boys in warm climates</b><br>Features: <b>giant cobblestone papillae</b> on upper tarsal conjunctiva, thick ropy mucus discharge, Horner-Trantas dots (limbal gelatinous nodules = degenerated eosinophils)<br><b>Shield ulcer</b> — sterile corneal ulcer from mechanical trauma of papillae<br>More severe than seasonal allergic conjunctivitis, may require topical cyclosporine or steroids." },
{ q: "What causes inclusion conjunctivitis and what serotypes are responsible?", a: "<b>Chlamydia trachomatis serotypes D–K</b><br>Sexually transmitted — occurs in sexually active young adults<br>Often associated with concurrent <b>chlamydial urethritis/cervicitis</b><br>Features: <b>chronic follicular conjunctivitis</b>, mucopurulent discharge, preauricular lymphadenopathy<br>Superior tarsal <b>follicular reaction</b>" },
{ q: "How is chlamydial conjunctivitis diagnosed?", a: "• <b>Giemsa stain</b> of conjunctival scrapings → <b>basophilic intracytoplasmic inclusion bodies</b> (Halberstaedter-Prowazek bodies) within epithelial cells<br>• <b>NAAT (nucleic acid amplification test)</b> — most sensitive and specific<br>• Direct fluorescent antibody (DFA) staining<br>• Chlamydial culture (McCoy cells)" },
{ q: "What is the treatment for chlamydial inclusion conjunctivitis?", a: "<b>Systemic antibiotics</b> (topical alone is insufficient):<br>• <b>Azithromycin 1g PO × 1 dose</b> (preferred)<br>• OR <b>Doxycycline 100mg PO BID × 7 days</b><br>Must <b>treat sexual partners</b><br>Test for co-infection with gonorrhea, syphilis, HIV<br>Topical erythromycin may be added but is adjunctive only." },
{ q: "What is trachoma and which serotypes cause it?", a: "<b>Trachoma</b> is caused by <b>Chlamydia trachomatis serotypes A, B, Ba, C</b>.<br>It is the <b>leading infectious cause of preventable blindness worldwide</b>.<br>Endemic in sub-Saharan Africa, South Asia, Middle East.<br>Spread by direct contact, flies, fomites (shared towels/cloths).<br>Repeated reinfection → progressive scarring → blindness." },
{ q: "Describe the stages and clinical features of trachoma.", a: "WHO classification:<br><b>TF</b> — Trachomatous inflammation, Follicular (≥5 follicles on upper tarsal conjunctiva)<br><b>TI</b> — Trachomatous inflammation, Intense (papillary hypertrophy obscuring tarsal vessels)<br><b>TS</b> — Trachomatous Scarring (<b>Arlt's line</b> = horizontal white scar lines on tarsal conjunctiva)<br><b>TT</b> — Trachomatous Trichiasis (in-turned lashes abrading cornea)<br><b>CO</b> — Corneal Opacity (leading to blindness)<br>Also: <b>Herbert's pits</b> (limbal scarring), <b>pannus</b> (superior corneal vascularization)" },
{ q: "What is the SAFE strategy for trachoma?", a: "<b>S</b> — Surgery for trichiasis (eyelid rotation)<br><b>A</b> — Antibiotics (azithromycin mass distribution)<br><b>F</b> — Facial cleanliness<br><b>E</b> — Environmental improvement (sanitation, water supply)<br>WHO-endorsed community-based intervention strategy." },
{ q: "What are Herbert's pits and Arlt's line?", a: "<b>Herbert's pits</b>: small depressions at the <b>superior limbus</b> — scarred-out follicles, pathognomonic of trachoma<br><b>Arlt's line</b>: linear horizontal <b>fibrous scars</b> on the <b>upper tarsal conjunctiva</b> — result of chronic trachomatous inflammation<br>Both are late findings of trachoma (C. trachomatis A–C)." },
{ q: "What is a pannus?", a: "A <b>pannus</b> is a fibrovascular membrane extending from the limbus onto the cornea.<br>In <b>trachoma</b>: typically <b>superior pannus</b><br>Causes: trachoma, contact lens overwear, rosacea keratitis<br>It consists of blood vessels + inflammatory infiltrate growing between Bowman's layer and epithelium.<br>Can lead to corneal opacification and vision loss." },
{ q: "What is ophthalmia neonatorum?", a: "<b>Ophthalmia neonatorum</b> = conjunctivitis occurring in the <b>first 28 days of life</b>.<br>It is a <b>notifiable condition</b>.<br>Acquired during passage through an infected birth canal (except chemical).<br>Key to differential: <b>timing of onset</b>." },
{ q: "Differentiate causes of ophthalmia neonatorum by timeline.", a: "<b>Day 1 (within 24h)</b>: Chemical — silver nitrate prophylaxis (rarely used now)<br><b>Days 2–5</b>: <b>N. gonorrhoeae</b> — purulent, ophthalmologic emergency, risk of perforation<br><b>Days 5–14</b>: <b>C. trachomatis</b> — most common infectious cause, mucopurulent<br><b>Days 6–14</b>: <b>HSV</b> — vesicular lid lesions, may cause keratitis/systemic disease<br><b>Days 5–28</b>: Other bacteria (S. aureus, E. coli)" },
{ q: "How is gonococcal ophthalmia neonatorum treated?", a: "<b>Systemic</b>: <b>Ceftriaxone 25–50 mg/kg IV/IM × 1 dose</b> (or cefotaxime if hyperbilirubinemia)<br><b>Topical</b>: saline irrigation + erythromycin ointment<br><b>Urgent ophthalmology evaluation</b><br>Both parents need evaluation and treatment for STI<br><i>Admission for systemic antibiotics — untreated can cause corneal perforation and blindness within 24 hours.</i>" },
{ q: "How is chlamydial ophthalmia neonatorum treated?", a: "<b>Oral erythromycin 50 mg/kg/day divided QID × 14 days</b><br>(Risk of <b>infantile hypertrophic pyloric stenosis</b> — counsel parents on warning signs)<br>Topical therapy alone is <b>insufficient</b> and does not prevent chlamydial pneumonia<br>Evaluate and treat parents for chlamydia." },
{ q: "What is the current standard prophylaxis for ophthalmia neonatorum?", a: "<b>Erythromycin 0.5% ophthalmic ointment</b> applied to both eyes within 1 hour of birth.<br>Effective against <b>gonococcal</b> ophthalmia (less reliable for chlamydial).<br>Replaced silver nitrate (which caused chemical conjunctivitis and was not effective against chlamydia).<br>Required by law in most US states." },
{ q: "What is the #1 risk factor for bacterial keratitis?", a: "<b>Contact lens wear</b> — especially extended/overnight wear, poor hygiene, sleeping in lenses<br>Other risk factors:<br>• Corneal trauma/surgery<br>• Dry eye<br>• Immunosuppression<br>• Topical corticosteroid use<br>• Lid abnormalities (lagophthalmos, entropion)" },
{ q: "What organism is most commonly associated with contact lens–related bacterial keratitis?", a: "<b>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</b><br>Features:<br>• Rapidly progressive corneal ulcer<br>• <b>Green or blue-green mucopurulent discharge</b><br>• Risk of <b>corneal perforation within 24–48 hours</b><br>• Hypopyon (layered WBCs in anterior chamber)<br>Requires immediate aggressive topical antibiotics." },
{ q: "What are the clinical features of a bacterial corneal ulcer?", a: "• <b>Pain, photophobia, tearing, decreased vision</b><br>• <b>White/yellow corneal infiltrate</b> (stromal opacity)<br>• <b>Epithelial defect</b> — stains with fluorescein<br>• <b>Hypopyon</b> — layer of WBCs in anterior chamber<br>• Mucopurulent discharge<br>• Lid edema, conjunctival injection<br>• <b>Ciliary flush</b>" },
{ q: "How is a corneal ulcer diagnosed?", a: "• <b>Slit-lamp examination</b> — assess size, depth, location of infiltrate<br>• <b>Fluorescein staining</b> — highlights epithelial defect (green under cobalt blue light)<br>• <b>Corneal scraping</b> for Gram stain, culture (blood agar, chocolate agar, Sabouraud agar for fungi, thioglycolate broth)<br>Culture before starting antibiotics for <b>central, large, or sight-threatening ulcers</b>." },
{ q: "What is the initial empiric treatment for bacterial corneal ulcer?", a: "Empiric <b>broad-spectrum topical therapy</b>:<br>• <b>Fortified vancomycin (25–50 mg/mL)</b> + <b>fortified tobramycin (14 mg/mL)</b> — alternating Q30min–1h<br>OR<br>• <b>Fluoroquinolone monotherapy</b> (moxifloxacin 0.5%, gatifloxacin) — Q30min–1h for small-moderate ulcers<br><b>Never patch</b> a potentially infected corneal ulcer (creates warm moist environment → worsens infection).<br>No topical steroids initially." },
{ q: "Why should you never patch an eye with a suspected corneal infection?", a: "Patching creates a <b>warm, dark, moist environment</b> that promotes bacterial proliferation, especially <b>Pseudomonas</b>.<br>It also prevents instillation of topical antibiotics.<br>Rule: <b>Never patch a contact lens–related or potentially infected corneal ulcer.</b>" },
{ q: "What causes HSV keratitis and what is the pathognomonic finding?", a: "Caused by <b>HSV-1</b> (most common) or HSV-2.<br>Pathognomonic finding: <b>Dendritic ulcer</b> — branching, tree-like epithelial lesion with terminal bulbs, best seen with <b>fluorescein staining</b> under cobalt blue light.<br>Rose bengal stains devitalized cells at ulcer margins." },
{ q: "What are the clinical features of HSV keratitis?", a: "• <b>Unilateral</b> red eye with pain, photophobia, tearing<br>• <b>Dendritic ulcer</b> on fluorescein staining<br>• <b>Decreased corneal sensation</b> (pathognomonic — test with wisp of cotton)<br>• History of recurrent episodes, triggered by stress, UV, fever<br>• May have skin vesicles on eyelid<br>• Recurrences can lead to <b>stromal keratitis</b> (immune-mediated → scarring → vision loss)" },
{ q: "What is the treatment for HSV epithelial keratitis?", a: "<b>Topical antivirals</b>:<br>• <b>Ganciclovir 0.15% gel</b> (Zirgan) — 5×/day<br>• <b>Trifluridine 1% drops</b> — Q2h (9×/day), taper over 2–3 weeks<br><b>Oral</b>: <b>Acyclovir 400mg 5×/day</b> or <b>valacyclovir 500mg TID</b> — especially for recurrence or stromal disease<br><b>Debridement</b> of dendritic epithelium may be performed." },
{ q: "Why should topical steroids NEVER be used alone in HSV epithelial keratitis?", a: "Steroids <b>suppress the immune response</b>, allowing <b>unchecked viral replication</b>.<br>This causes:<br>• Dendritic → <b>geographic ulcer</b> (enlarged, amoeboid-shaped)<br>• Deeper corneal involvement<br>• Corneal perforation<br>• Vision loss<br><b>Rule</b>: Never use steroids without concurrent antiviral coverage in HSV keratitis.<br><i>Steroids + antiviral can be used together for HSV stromal keratitis (immune-mediated).</i>" },
{ q: "Differentiate HSV dendritic ulcer from HZV pseudodendrite.", a: "<b>HSV dendrite</b>: true branching with <b>terminal bulbs</b>, central ulceration stains with fluorescein, borders stain with rose bengal<br><b>HZV pseudodendrite</b>: elevated mucous plaque <b>without terminal bulbs</b>, does not have true ulceration, stains poorly with fluorescein<br>HSV → <b>topical antivirals essential</b><br>HZV → treated with <b>oral antivirals (valacyclovir)</b>" },
{ q: "What is the Hutchinson sign and why is it important?", a: "<b>Hutchinson sign</b>: vesicular lesions on the <b>tip, side, or root of the nose</b>.<br>Indicates involvement of the <b>nasociliary branch of V1 (ophthalmic division of trigeminal nerve)</b>.<br>This branch also supplies the eye → strong predictor of <b>ocular involvement</b> in herpes zoster ophthalmicus (HZO).<br><b>50–72% of patients with Hutchinson sign develop ocular complications</b> (keratitis, uveitis, glaucoma)." },
{ q: "How is herpes zoster ophthalmicus (HZO) treated?", a: "<b>Oral antivirals</b> (within 72h of rash onset):<br>• <b>Valacyclovir 1g PO TID × 7 days</b> (preferred)<br>• OR Acyclovir 800mg PO 5×/day × 7–10 days<br>• OR Famciclovir 500mg PO TID × 7 days<br><b>Pain management</b>: analgesics, gabapentin for postherpetic neuralgia<br>If ocular involvement: <b>ophthalmology referral</b> + topical steroids (with antiviral cover) for keratitis/uveitis." },
{ q: "What organisms cause fungal keratitis and what is the classic risk factor?", a: "<b>Filamentous fungi</b>: <b>Aspergillus, Fusarium</b> — classic risk factor: <b>trauma with vegetable/organic matter</b> (tree branch, thorn) in tropical/agricultural settings<br><b>Yeast</b>: <b>Candida</b> — associated with chronic ocular surface disease, immunosuppression, topical steroid use<br>Also: contact lens wear (especially with Fusarium)" },
{ q: "What are the characteristic features of a fungal corneal ulcer?", a: "• <b>Satellite lesions</b> (small infiltrates surrounding main ulcer) — hallmark<br>• <b>Feathery, irregular borders</b> with elevated edges<br>• Gray-white, dry-looking infiltrate<br>• <b>Endothelial plaque</b><br>• Hypopyon (often thick, immobile)<br>• Indolent, slow progression<br>• <b>Worsening despite antibacterial therapy</b>" },
{ q: "How is fungal keratitis treated?", a: "<b>Filamentous fungi</b>: <b>Natamycin 5% topical</b> — first-line<br><b>Yeast (Candida)</b>: <b>Amphotericin B 0.15% topical</b><br><b>Alternative</b>: <b>Voriconazole</b> (topical 1% or oral) — broad-spectrum, good penetration<br>Treatment is prolonged (weeks to months)<br>Corneal scraping/culture essential for diagnosis (Sabouraud agar, calcofluor white stain).<br>Consider <b>therapeutic keratoplasty</b> if perforation threatens." },
{ q: "What is Acanthamoeba keratitis and what is the classic risk factor?", a: "<b>Acanthamoeba keratitis</b> is a rare, severe parasitic keratitis caused by the free-living amoeba <b>Acanthamoeba</b>.<br>Classic risk: <b>contact lens use + exposure to tap/pool/hot tub water</b><br>Also: homemade saline solutions, showering/swimming with lenses<br>Organism exists as trophozoites and resistant <b>cysts</b> (difficult to eradicate)." },
{ q: "What are the clinical features of Acanthamoeba keratitis?", a: "• <b>Severe pain out of proportion to clinical findings</b> — hallmark<br>• <b>Ring-shaped stromal infiltrate</b> (ring infiltrate/abscess) — pathognomonic<br>• <b>Perineural infiltrates</b> (radial keratoneuritis) — early sign<br>• Epithelial irregularity/pseudodendrite (may mimic HSV)<br>• Waxing and waning course<br>• Resistant to standard antibiotics and antivirals" },
{ q: "How is Acanthamoeba keratitis treated?", a: "Prolonged topical combination therapy (months):<br>• <b>PHMB (polyhexamethylene biguanide) 0.02%</b> or <b>chlorhexidine 0.02%</b><br>• PLUS <b>propamidine 0.1% (Brolene)</b> or hexamidine<br>Initially Q1h around the clock, then slowly taper<br>Duration: <b>3–6+ months minimum</b> (to kill cysts)<br>Oral voriconazole may be added in severe cases<br>May eventually need <b>corneal transplant</b> for scarring." },
{ q: "What is anterior uveitis (iritis) and what are its key symptoms?", a: "<b>Anterior uveitis</b> = inflammation of the iris (iritis) ± ciliary body (iridocyclitis).<br>Key symptoms:<br>• <b>Unilateral eye pain</b> (deep, aching)<br>• <b>Photophobia</b><br>• <b>Consensual photophobia</b> (pain when light shone in the OTHER eye — pathognomonic)<br>• <b>Blurred vision</b><br>• <b>Tearing</b> (no discharge)<br>• <b>Ciliary flush</b>" },
{ q: "Why does consensual photophobia occur in anterior uveitis?", a: "Light shone in the <b>unaffected eye</b> causes a <b>consensual pupillary constriction</b> in the <b>inflamed eye</b>.<br>Constriction of the inflamed iris → <b>movement of the inflamed iris tissue</b> → pain.<br>This is <b>pathognomonic for anterior uveitis</b> and distinguishes it from other causes of red eye." },
{ q: "What findings are seen on slit-lamp examination in anterior uveitis?", a: "• <b>Cells</b> — WBCs floating in aqueous humor (Tyndall effect)<br>• <b>Flare</b> — protein-rich exudate making the aqueous beam visible (like a shaft of light in a dusty room)<br>• <b>Keratic precipitates (KPs)</b> — WBC deposits on corneal endothelium<br>• <b>Posterior synechiae</b> — adhesions of iris to anterior lens capsule<br>• Miotic pupil<br>• ± <b>Hypopyon</b> (layered WBCs in AC)" },
{ q: "Differentiate fine (non-granulomatous) from mutton-fat (granulomatous) keratic precipitates.", a: "<b>Fine KPs (non-granulomatous)</b>:<br>• Small, dust-like deposits<br>• Lymphocytes/PMNs<br>• Seen in: HLA-B27 uveitis, idiopathic<br><br><b>Mutton-fat KPs (granulomatous)</b>:<br>• Large, greasy, yellowish deposits<br>• Macrophages/epithelioid cells<br>• Seen in: <b>sarcoidosis, TB, syphilis, VKH syndrome, sympathetic ophthalmia</b>" },
{ q: "What are posterior synechiae and why are they dangerous?", a: "<b>Posterior synechiae</b> = adhesions between the <b>iris and anterior lens capsule</b>.<br>Dangers:<br>• <b>Irregular (festooned) pupil</b> — won't dilate fully<br>• If 360° (seclusio pupillae) → blocks aqueous flow → <b>iris bombé</b> → <b>secondary angle-closure glaucoma</b><br>Prevention: early use of <b>cycloplegics (atropine)</b> to dilate pupil and break/prevent synechiae." },
{ q: "What is the treatment of anterior uveitis?", a: "<b>Cycloplegics</b>: <b>Atropine 1%</b> or cyclopentolate 1% drops — paralyze ciliary body (↓ pain), dilate pupil (prevent/break posterior synechiae)<br><b>Topical corticosteroids</b>: <b>Prednisolone acetate 1%</b> — aggressive initial dosing (Q1–2h), slow taper<br>Treat underlying cause if identified<br><b>Never stop steroids abruptly</b> — rebound inflammation<br>Monitor IOP (steroids can cause glaucoma)" },
{ q: "What is the most common systemic association with anterior uveitis?", a: "<b>HLA-B27</b>–associated diseases:<br>• <b>Ankylosing spondylitis</b> (most common systemic association)<br>• <b>Reactive arthritis</b> (formerly Reiter syndrome: \"can't see, can't pee, can't climb a tree\")<br>• <b>Inflammatory bowel disease</b> (Crohn, UC)<br>• <b>Psoriatic arthritis</b><br>Uveitis is typically <b>acute, recurrent, unilateral</b> (alternating), <b>non-granulomatous</b>." },
{ q: "Describe the classic triad of reactive arthritis and its ocular manifestation.", a: "Classic triad: <b>Conjunctivitis</b> (most common ocular), <b>urethritis</b>, <b>arthritis</b><br>Mnemonic: \"Can't see, can't pee, can't climb a tree\"<br>May also develop <b>anterior uveitis</b> (more specific than conjunctivitis)<br>HLA-B27 positive<br>Triggered by GI (Shigella, Salmonella, Yersinia, Campylobacter) or GU (Chlamydia) infection." },
{ q: "How does sarcoidosis present in the eye?", a: "<b>Bilateral granulomatous uveitis</b> — most common ocular manifestation<br>Features: <b>mutton-fat KPs</b>, iris nodules (<b>Koeppe</b> = pupillary margin, <b>Busacca</b> = iris stroma), posterior synechiae<br>May also have <b>lacrimal gland enlargement</b>, <b>band keratopathy</b> (calcium deposits in Bowman's layer)<br>Can affect any part of the uvea (anterior > posterior > panuveitis)<br>Labs: elevated <b>ACE</b>, bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy on CXR, non-caseating granulomas on biopsy." },
{ q: "What are Koeppe and Busacca nodules?", a: "<b>Koeppe nodules</b>: small white nodules at the <b>pupillary margin</b> of the iris<br><b>Busacca nodules</b>: larger nodules within the <b>iris stroma</b> (not at pupillary margin)<br>Both are <b>granulomatous</b> collections (epithelioid cells + giant cells).<br>Classic finding in <b>sarcoidosis</b>-associated uveitis.<br>Also seen in TB, syphilitic, and VKH uveitis." },
{ q: "How does juvenile idiopathic arthritis (JIA)-associated uveitis present?", a: "Most common in: <b>oligoarticular (pauciarticular) JIA</b>, <b>ANA-positive</b>, <b>young girls</b><br>Uveitis is <b>CHRONIC, BILATERAL, ASYMPTOMATIC</b> (white eye!) — often detected only by screening<br><b>Non-granulomatous</b> anterior uveitis<br>Complications: band keratopathy, posterior synechiae, cataracts, glaucoma, amblyopia<br><b>Regular slit-lamp screening every 3 months</b> is essential." },
{ q: "Why is JIA-associated uveitis dangerous despite being asymptomatic?", a: "Because it is <b>chronic and painless</b>, it can progress silently to:<br>• <b>Band keratopathy</b> (calcium deposits)<br>• <b>Posterior synechiae → secondary glaucoma</b><br>• <b>Cataract</b><br>• <b>Cystoid macular edema</b><br>• <b>Amblyopia</b> (in young children)<br>Without screening, children may present with <b>irreversible vision loss</b>.<br>All JIA patients (especially ANA+ pauciarticular girls) need <b>routine slit-lamp exams</b>." },
{ q: "What is the ocular hallmark of Behçet disease?", a: "<b>Hypopyon uveitis</b> — a shifting hypopyon (WBC layering in AC) that moves with changes in head position.<br>Behçet disease: recurrent oral ulcers + genital ulcers + uveitis<br>Also causes: retinal vasculitis (occlusive), optic neuritis<br>Most common in young men along the Silk Road (Turkey, Middle East, Japan).<br>Associated with <b>HLA-B51</b><br>Treatment: colchicine, azathioprine, anti-TNF agents" },
{ q: "What is band keratopathy?", a: "<b>Band keratopathy</b> = horizontal band-shaped <b>calcium deposition</b> in <b>Bowman's layer</b> and superficial stroma, typically in the interpalpebral fissure.<br>Associations:<br>• <b>Chronic uveitis</b> (especially JIA)<br>• Sarcoidosis<br>• Hypercalcemia/hyperphosphatemia<br>• Chronic ocular disease<br>Treatment: chelation with <b>EDTA</b> or excimer laser." },
{ q: "What infections can cause uveitis?", a: "<b>Anterior</b>: HSV, HZV, syphilis, TB, Lyme disease<br><b>Posterior</b>: <b>Toxoplasmosis</b> (#1 worldwide), CMV, toxocara, TB, syphilis, candida<br><b>Panuveitis</b>: Syphilis (\"the great mimicker\"), TB, endophthalmitis<br>Key rule: <b>Syphilis can cause ANY form of uveitis</b> → always check RPR/VDRL + FTA-ABS in uveitis workup." },
{ q: "What is the 'headlight in the fog' sign?", a: "Classic fundoscopic finding in <b>toxoplasmic retinochoroiditis</b>:<br>An active <b>white/yellow retinal lesion</b> (necrotizing retinitis) seen through <b>overlying vitreous haze</b> (vitritis).<br>Looks like a headlight shining through fog.<br><b>Toxoplasmosis</b> is the #1 cause of posterior uveitis worldwide.<br>Often adjacent to an old <b>pigmented chorioretinal scar</b> (reactivation of congenital infection)." },
{ q: "How is ocular toxoplasmosis treated?", a: "Indicated for sight-threatening lesions (near macula/optic disc), severe vitritis, immunocompromised patients:<br>Classic triple therapy:<br>• <b>Pyrimethamine</b> + <b>sulfadiazine</b> + <b>folinic acid</b> (leucovorin)<br>Alternative: <b>TMP-SMX</b> or clindamycin + pyrimethamine<br>Add <b>systemic corticosteroids</b> (after 24–48h of antimicrobials) for severe inflammation.<br>Duration: 4–6 weeks." },
{ q: "What is the characteristic fundus appearance in CMV retinitis?", a: "<b>Pizza-pie or cottage cheese and ketchup fundus</b>:<br>• <b>Yellow-white fluffy retinal lesions</b> (cottage cheese = areas of retinal necrosis)<br>• <b>Retinal hemorrhages</b> (ketchup)<br>• Perivascular distribution, advancing brush-fire border<br>Seen in <b>severely immunocompromised</b> patients (CD4 < 50 in HIV/AIDS)<br>Can cause retinal detachment" },
{ q: "How is CMV retinitis treated?", a: "<b>First-line</b>: <b>Valganciclovir</b> (oral) or <b>ganciclovir</b> (IV) — induction then maintenance<br><b>Alternatives</b>: foscarnet (for ganciclovir-resistant, nephrotoxic), cidofovir<br><b>Intravitreal ganciclovir</b> injections or implant for severe/refractory cases<br>Most important: <b>Immune reconstitution with ART</b> (HAART) — allows eventual discontinuation of anti-CMV therapy when CD4 > 100 for 3–6 months." },
{ q: "What is intermediate uveitis?", a: "<b>Intermediate uveitis</b> = inflammation primarily of the <b>vitreous</b> and peripheral retina (pars plana).<br>Subtypes: <b>Pars planitis</b> (idiopathic, most common), vitritis<br>Features: <b>floaters</b>, blurred vision, <b>snowbanking</b> (exudate over pars plana), <b>snowballs</b> (vitreous opacities)<br>Associated with <b>multiple sclerosis</b> (especially in young adults)<br>Treatment: periocular/intravitreal steroids, systemic immunosuppression if bilateral/severe." },
{ q: "What is snowbanking and what does it indicate?", a: "<b>Snowbanking</b> = a white-yellow exudate/fibrovascular tissue deposited over the <b>inferior pars plana</b> and peripheral retina.<br>It is the hallmark finding of <b>pars planitis</b> (a form of intermediate uveitis).<br>Best seen with <b>indirect ophthalmoscopy</b> with scleral depression.<br>Associated with <b>multiple sclerosis</b> — consider MRI brain in young adults with pars planitis." },
{ q: "What is the difference between scleritis and episcleritis?", a: "<b>Episcleritis</b>:<br>• Mild discomfort, not severe pain<br>• <b>Sectoral injection</b>, bright red, blanches with phenylephrine 2.5%<br>• Self-limiting (1–2 weeks)<br>• Rarely associated with systemic disease<br>• Treatment: artificial tears, topical NSAIDs<br><br><b>Scleritis</b>:<br>• <b>Severe, boring pain</b> (wakes from sleep, radiates to jaw/temple)<br>• Deep violaceous injection, does NOT blanch with phenylephrine<br>• <b>Scleral edema/thinning</b><br>• Associated with <b>serious systemic disease</b> (50%)<br>• Sight-threatening<br>• Treatment: systemic NSAIDs/steroids/immunosuppressants" },
{ q: "What systemic diseases are associated with scleritis?", a: "• <b>Rheumatoid arthritis</b> (most common association)<br>• <b>Granulomatosis with polyangiitis (Wegener's)</b><br>• <b>Relapsing polychondritis</b><br>• Systemic lupus erythematosus<br>• Polyarteritis nodosa<br>• Inflammatory bowel disease<br>• Ankylosing spondylitis<br>About <b>50% of scleritis patients have an underlying systemic autoimmune disease</b>." },
{ q: "What is the most severe form of scleritis?", a: "<b>Necrotizing scleritis</b> — the most severe and destructive form.<br>Types:<br>• <b>With inflammation</b> — intense pain, scleral necrosis, high risk of perforation<br>• <b>Without inflammation (scleromalacia perforans)</b> — painless scleral thinning, seen in <b>long-standing RA</b>, uveal tissue visible through thin sclera<br>Both require aggressive systemic immunosuppression (cyclophosphamide, rituximab)." },
{ q: "How is episcleritis treated?", a: "<b>Usually self-limiting</b> (1–2 weeks):<br>• <b>Artificial tears/cold compresses</b><br>• <b>Topical NSAIDs</b> (ketorolac) if symptomatic<br>• Oral NSAIDs for more persistent cases<br>Rarely requires topical steroids.<br>No systemic workup typically needed unless recurrent." },
{ q: "What is the phenylephrine blanching test?", a: "Topical <b>phenylephrine 2.5–10%</b> is applied to the eye:<br>• <b>Conjunctival vessels</b> → blanch (superficial)<br>• <b>Episcleral vessels</b> → blanch with higher concentration<br>• <b>Scleral vessels</b> → do NOT blanch<br>Used to differentiate:<br>• <b>Episcleritis</b> (vessels blanch) from <b>scleritis</b> (deep vessels do not blanch)<br>• Conjunctivitis from deeper inflammation" },
{ q: "What is a subconjunctival hemorrhage?", a: "A <b>bright red, well-demarcated, painless</b> area of hemorrhage beneath the conjunctiva.<br>Features: <b>no pain, no discharge, no vision changes</b><br>Causes: spontaneous (Valsalva — cough, sneeze, straining), minor trauma, anticoagulant use, hypertension<br>Treatment: <b>None — self-resolves in 2–3 weeks</b><br>If recurrent: <b>check blood pressure</b>, bleeding disorders, coagulation studies" },
{ q: "When should you be concerned about a subconjunctival hemorrhage?", a: "• <b>Recurrent episodes</b> — check BP, coagulation studies<br>• <b>Bilateral</b> — consider bleeding disorder<br>• <b>Associated with trauma</b> — rule out globe rupture (scleral laceration may be hidden underneath)<br>• <b>360° (circumferential)</b> in trauma — high suspicion for <b>occult globe rupture</b><br>• Post-surgical — may indicate wound leak" },
{ q: "What is a corneal abrasion and how does it present?", a: "A <b>traumatic defect of the corneal epithelium</b>.<br>Symptoms: sudden onset <b>pain, foreign body sensation</b>, tearing, photophobia, blurred vision<br>Diagnosis: <b>fluorescein staining</b> — defect glows bright green under cobalt blue light<br>Always <b>evert the eyelid</b> to look for a foreign body under the upper lid." },
{ q: "How is a corneal abrasion treated?", a: "• <b>Topical antibiotics</b> prophylaxis: erythromycin ointment or fluoroquinolone drops QID<br>• <b>Cycloplegic drops</b> (cyclopentolate) for pain relief (reduces ciliary spasm)<br>• Oral analgesics (NSAIDs, acetaminophen)<br>• Topical NSAIDs (ketorolac) for pain<br>• <b>NO patching for contact lens wearers</b> (risk of Pseudomonas)<br>• Follow up in 24–48 hours<br>• Most heal in <b>24–72 hours</b>" },
{ q: "What is a pterygium and what causes it?", a: "A <b>wing-shaped fibrovascular growth</b> of conjunctival tissue extending onto the <b>cornea</b>.<br>Typically on the <b>nasal side</b>.<br>Risk factors: <b>UV light exposure</b>, wind, dust (outdoor workers, near equator)<br>Symptoms: irritation, foreign body sensation, cosmetic concern<br>Can cause <b>astigmatism</b> and visual impairment if it encroaches on the visual axis." },
{ q: "How is a pterygium treated?", a: "<b>Conservative</b>: artificial tears, sunglasses (UV protection)<br><b>Surgical excision</b> is indicated if:<br>• Encroaching on <b>visual axis</b><br>• Causing significant <b>astigmatism</b><br>• Cosmetically bothersome<br>• Restricting ocular motility<br>Recurrence is common (~30–40%) with bare sclera technique.<br><b>Conjunctival autograft</b> or <b>mitomycin C</b> reduces recurrence." },
{ q: "How does a pterygium differ from a pinguecula?", a: "<b>Pterygium</b>: fibrovascular growth that <b>crosses the limbus</b> onto the cornea, wing-shaped, nasal > temporal<br><b>Pinguecula</b>: yellowish, slightly elevated deposit on the <b>conjunctiva only</b> — does NOT cross the limbus<br>Both associated with UV exposure.<br>Pinguecula: observation, artificial tears<br>Pterygium: surgery if encroaching on visual axis" },
{ q: "What is a pinguecula composed of?", a: "A pinguecula consists of <b>elastotic degeneration</b> of conjunctival collagen:<br>• Hyaline and elastic tissue deposits<br>• <b>Actinic (UV) damage</b> to subepithelial collagen<br>Located on the bulbar conjunctiva in the <b>interpalpebral fissure</b> (nasal > temporal).<br>It can become inflamed (<b>pingueculitis</b>) — treat with topical NSAIDs or mild steroids." },
{ q: "What is the differential diagnosis for a hypopyon?", a: "<b>Hypopyon</b> = layered WBCs in the anterior chamber.<br>Differential:<br>• <b>Severe bacterial keratitis</b> (corneal ulcer)<br>• <b>Endophthalmitis</b> (postoperative or endogenous)<br>• <b>Behçet disease</b> (shifting, sterile hypopyon)<br>• <b>HLA-B27 anterior uveitis</b> (severe cases)<br>• <b>Masquerade syndromes</b>: retinoblastoma (children), leukemia, lymphoma (pseudohypopyon — tumor cells)" },
{ q: "What is the workup for a first episode of anterior uveitis?", a: "Minimal workup for a first episode in a young, healthy patient (most are idiopathic).<br>For recurrent, bilateral, or granulomatous uveitis:<br>• <b>HLA-B27</b><br>• <b>CBC, ESR, CRP</b><br>• <b>CXR</b> (sarcoidosis, TB)<br>• <b>RPR/VDRL + FTA-ABS</b> (syphilis — always rule out)<br>• <b>ACE level</b> (sarcoidosis)<br>• <b>PPD or QuantiFERON-Gold</b> (TB)<br>• <b>ANA</b> (JIA in children, lupus)" },
{ q: "A patient presents with acute red eye, severe pain, halos around lights, nausea/vomiting, and a mid-dilated fixed pupil. What is the diagnosis?", a: "<b>Acute angle-closure glaucoma</b><br>Features: severely elevated IOP (>40 mmHg), corneal edema (hazy), <b>mid-dilated, fixed pupil</b>, shallow anterior chamber<br>Treatment: <b>topical timolol + pilocarpine + apraclonidine + IV acetazolamide + IV mannitol</b><br>Definitive: <b>laser peripheral iridotomy</b> (both eyes — fellow eye is prophylactic)" },
{ q: "Compare the pupil findings in acute glaucoma vs anterior uveitis.", a: "<b>Acute angle-closure glaucoma</b>: <b>mid-dilated, fixed, unreactive</b> pupil<br><b>Anterior uveitis</b>: <b>miotic (constricted)</b> pupil due to inflammatory spasm of sphincter<br>This is a key differentiating feature on physical exam." },
{ q: "What type of discharge suggests each category of conjunctivitis?", a: "<b>Mucopurulent (thick yellow-green)</b> → Bacterial<br><b>Copious purulent (overflowing pus)</b> → Gonococcal (hyperacute)<br><b>Watery (serous)</b> → Viral<br><b>Stringy/mucoid + watery</b> → Allergic<br><b>Mucopurulent + chronic</b> → Chlamydial" },
{ q: "What is chemosis?", a: "<b>Chemosis</b> = edema (swelling) of the <b>conjunctiva</b>, creating a watery, gelatinous, balloon-like appearance.<br>The conjunctiva may even prolapse over the lid margin.<br>Causes:<br>• <b>Allergic conjunctivitis</b> (most common)<br>• Anaphylaxis<br>• Orbital cellulitis<br>• Cavernous sinus thrombosis<br>• Thyroid eye disease" },
{ q: "What is endophthalmitis and how does it present?", a: "<b>Endophthalmitis</b> = infection of the intraocular fluids (vitreous and/or aqueous).<br><b>Exogenous</b> (most common): post-cataract surgery (S. epidermidis #1), post-intravitreal injection, post-trauma<br><b>Endogenous</b>: hematogenous spread (Candida, S. aureus — IV drug use, immunocompromised)<br>Presents: <b>pain, ↓↓ vision, hypopyon, lid edema, absent red reflex</b><br>Treatment: <b>intravitreal antibiotics (vancomycin + ceftazidime)</b> ± vitrectomy" },
{ q: "What organisms are most common in post-surgical endophthalmitis?", a: "<b>Acute</b> (within days): <b>Staphylococcus epidermidis</b> (#1), S. aureus, Streptococcus spp.<br><b>Delayed/chronic</b> (weeks-months): <b>Propionibacterium acnes</b> (now Cutibacterium), fungal<br>Post-trauma: <b>Bacillus cereus</b> (fulminant, poor prognosis)<br>Treatment: intravitreal vancomycin + ceftazidime ± pars plana vitrectomy (per EVS guidelines if vision is LP or worse)." },
{ q: "What is the Endophthalmitis Vitrectomy Study (EVS) key finding?", a: "The <b>EVS</b> (1995) studied post-cataract surgery endophthalmitis:<br>Key finding: <b>Immediate vitrectomy</b> improved outcomes only when presenting vision was <b>light perception (LP) or worse</b>.<br>For patients with <b>better than LP vision</b>: vitreous tap/biopsy + intravitreal antibiotics was equally effective.<br>Systemic antibiotics (IV) did <b>NOT</b> improve outcomes." },
{ q: "What is sympathetic ophthalmia?", a: "<b>Sympathetic ophthalmia</b> = bilateral <b>granulomatous panuveitis</b> occurring after <b>penetrating trauma or surgery</b> to one eye.<br>The injured eye = <b>exciting eye</b>; the fellow eye = <b>sympathizing eye</b>.<br>Mechanism: autoimmune response to <b>uveal/retinal antigens</b> exposed by trauma.<br>Onset: 2 weeks to years after injury (most within 3 months).<br>Treatment: aggressive immunosuppression (steroids + steroid-sparing agents). Enucleation of exciting eye considered if done <b>within 2 weeks</b> of injury." },
{ q: "What is Vogt-Koyanagi-Harada (VKH) disease?", a: "<b>VKH</b> = bilateral granulomatous panuveitis with <b>exudative retinal detachments</b>.<br>Associated with: <b>poliosis</b> (whitening of eyelashes/hair), <b>vitiligo</b>, <b>alopecia</b>, <b>dysacusis</b> (hearing loss), meningeal signs<br>Autoimmune against <b>melanocytes</b><br>More common in: Asians, Hispanics, Native Americans<br>Histology: granulomatous inflammation similar to sympathetic ophthalmia but <b>no prior trauma</b><br>Treatment: high-dose systemic corticosteroids + immunosuppressive agents" },
{ q: "What is Fuchs heterochromic iridocyclitis?", a: "<b>Fuchs heterochromic iridocyclitis</b>: chronic, low-grade, <b>unilateral</b> anterior uveitis.<br>Features:<br>• <b>Heterochromia</b> — affected iris is <b>lighter</b> (stromal atrophy)<br>• Fine, diffuse, stellate KPs<br>• Minimal cells/flare, no posterior synechiae<br>• Complications: <b>cataract</b> (most common), secondary glaucoma<br>• Does NOT respond to steroids (steroids not recommended)<br>• Usually does not require treatment unless complications arise" },
{ q: "What is the most common cause of unilateral red eye with dendritic corneal ulcer and decreased corneal sensation?", a: "<b>HSV keratitis</b><br>Dendritic ulcer with terminal bulbs = pathognomonic<br>Decreased corneal sensation due to damage to corneal nerves by HSV<br>Test: cotton wisp test — compare both eyes<br>Treatment: topical ganciclovir or trifluridine + oral acyclovir/valacyclovir" },
{ q: "A contact lens wearer presents with severe pain out of proportion to exam, ring-shaped corneal infiltrate. Diagnosis?", a: "<b>Acanthamoeba keratitis</b><br>Key features: contact lens + tap water exposure, pain out of proportion, ring infiltrate, perineural infiltrates<br>Treatment: PHMB + propamidine (prolonged course, months)" },
{ q: "A farmer is hit in the eye by a tree branch and develops a corneal ulcer with satellite lesions and feathery borders. Diagnosis and treatment?", a: "<b>Fungal keratitis</b> (likely <b>Aspergillus or Fusarium</b>)<br>Key clues: organic/vegetable matter trauma, satellite lesions, feathery borders, slow progression<br>Treatment: <b>Natamycin 5% topical</b> (first-line for filamentous fungi)<br>Culture on Sabouraud dextrose agar" },
{ q: "What is the most common infectious cause of blindness worldwide?", a: "<b>Trachoma</b> — caused by <b>Chlamydia trachomatis serotypes A, B, Ba, C</b>.<br>Repeated infection → scarring (Arlt's line) → trichiasis → corneal opacification → blindness.<br>Prevention: <b>SAFE strategy</b> (Surgery, Antibiotics, Facial cleanliness, Environmental improvement)." },
{ q: "What organisms cause neonatal conjunctivitis at days 2–5 of life?", a: "<b>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</b> — onset at <b>2–5 days</b><br>Copious purulent discharge, lid swelling<br>Can rapidly perforate the cornea<br>Treatment: systemic ceftriaxone + topical erythromycin + saline irrigation<br>This is earlier than chlamydial (5–14 days) because gonococci have a shorter incubation period." },
{ q: "A newborn develops conjunctivitis at 10 days of life. Most likely cause?", a: "<b>Chlamydia trachomatis</b> (serotypes D–K)<br>Onset: <b>5–14 days of life</b> — most common infectious cause of ophthalmia neonatorum<br>Features: mucopurulent discharge, lid swelling, follicles are absent in neonates (immature lymphoid tissue)<br>Treatment: <b>oral erythromycin × 14 days</b> (prevents chlamydial pneumonia)" },
{ q: "Why is silver nitrate prophylaxis no longer preferred for ophthalmia neonatorum?", a: "Silver nitrate:<br>• Causes <b>chemical conjunctivitis</b> within 24 hours (most common cause of Day 1 neonatal conjunctivitis)<br>• <b>NOT effective against Chlamydia</b> trachomatis<br>Replaced by <b>erythromycin 0.5% ointment</b> — effective against both gonococcal and partially against chlamydial ophthalmia neonatorum." },
{ q: "What is the most common cause of conjunctivitis overall?", a: "<b>Adenovirus</b> — viral conjunctivitis is the most common cause of conjunctivitis in adults.<br>Features: watery discharge, follicular reaction, preauricular lymphadenopathy, highly contagious.<br>Self-limiting, supportive care only." },
{ q: "What finding on slit lamp confirms an anterior chamber reaction?", a: "<b>Cells and flare</b>:<br>• <b>Cells</b> = WBCs suspended in aqueous, visible as tiny white particles in a narrow slit beam<br>• <b>Flare</b> = protein leakage into aqueous, making the beam itself visible (Tyndall effect)<br>Graded 0 to 4+ based on density<br>Confirms <b>anterior uveitis/iritis</b>." },
{ q: "What is the role of fluorescein staining in red eye evaluation?", a: "<b>Fluorescein</b> is an orange dye that highlights <b>epithelial defects</b> on the cornea and conjunctiva.<br>Under <b>cobalt blue light</b>, dye pools in areas of missing epithelium → glows bright green.<br>Detects:<br>• Corneal abrasion<br>• Corneal ulcer<br>• HSV dendritic ulcer<br>• Contact lens-related erosion<br>• Seidel test positive = aqueous leak (penetrating injury)" },
{ q: "What is the Seidel test?", a: "The <b>Seidel test</b> detects <b>aqueous humor leakage</b> from a corneal/scleral wound (full-thickness perforation).<br>Technique: apply concentrated fluorescein to wound → observe under cobalt blue light.<br><b>Positive</b>: aqueous dilutes/washes away the fluorescein in a streaming pattern → <b>indicates open globe injury</b>.<br>Requires emergent ophthalmology consultation and surgical repair." },
{ q: "What is a corneal foreign body and how is it managed?", a: "Metallic, glass, or organic material embedded in the cornea.<br>Diagnosis: slit-lamp exam, evert upper eyelid to check for subtarsal foreign body<br>Management:<br>• Topical anesthesia (proparacaine)<br>• <b>Remove under slit lamp with needle or spud</b><br>• If metallic: remove <b>rust ring</b> with burr (can be deferred 24h)<br>• <b>Topical antibiotic</b> (fluoroquinolone) after removal<br>• Tetanus prophylaxis if not current" },
{ q: "What ocular condition is associated with rheumatoid arthritis?", a: "RA is associated with multiple ocular conditions:<br>• <b>Keratoconjunctivitis sicca (dry eyes)</b> — most common (Sjögren syndrome overlap)<br>• <b>Scleritis</b> (especially necrotizing → scleromalacia perforans)<br>• <b>Episcleritis</b><br>• Peripheral ulcerative keratitis (PUK) — corneal melt<br>RA is the <b>#1 systemic disease associated with scleritis</b>." },
{ q: "What is peripheral ulcerative keratitis (PUK)?", a: "<b>PUK</b> = progressive peripheral corneal thinning/ulceration at the limbus, often with overlying epithelial defect.<br>Associated with systemic autoimmune diseases:<br>• <b>Rheumatoid arthritis</b> (most common)<br>• Granulomatosis with polyangiitis (Wegener's)<br>• SLE, PAN<br>Can progress to <b>corneal perforation</b> (corneal melt).<br>Treatment: systemic immunosuppression (not just topical)." },
{ q: "A patient with ankylosing spondylitis presents with acute unilateral eye pain, photophobia, and ciliary flush. Most likely diagnosis?", a: "<b>Acute anterior uveitis (iritis)</b><br>Most common extra-articular manifestation of <b>ankylosing spondylitis</b><br>HLA-B27 associated, non-granulomatous, recurrent, alternates between eyes<br>Treatment: topical steroids + cycloplegics" },
{ q: "What is the difference between iritis, iridocyclitis, and panuveitis?", a: "<b>Iritis</b>: inflammation of the <b>iris only</b> (anterior chamber cells/flare)<br><b>Iridocyclitis</b>: inflammation of <b>iris + ciliary body</b> (anterior chamber + anterior vitreous cells)<br><b>Panuveitis</b>: inflammation of <b>entire uveal tract</b> (anterior chamber + vitreous + retina/choroid)<br>Anatomic classification: anterior, intermediate, posterior, panuveitis" },
{ q: "What drugs can cause uveitis as a side effect?", a: "Drug-induced uveitis:<br>• <b>Rifabutin</b> (used in MAC prophylaxis — bilateral, hypopyon)<br>• <b>Cidofovir</b> (used for CMV)<br>• <b>Bisphosphonates</b> (pamidronate, alendronate)<br>• <b>Sulfonamides</b><br>• <b>Checkpoint inhibitors</b> (ipilimumab, nivolumab)<br>• Fluoroquinolones (rare)<br>• TNF-alpha inhibitors (paradoxical)" },
{ q: "What is the role of cycloplegics in anterior uveitis treatment?", a: "<b>Cycloplegics</b> (atropine, cyclopentolate, homatropine) serve 2 purposes:<br>1. <b>Pain relief</b> — paralyze the ciliary muscle → reduce ciliary spasm<br>2. <b>Prevent/break posterior synechiae</b> — dilate the pupil to keep iris from adhering to lens capsule<br>Atropine (long-acting, 1–2 weeks) is preferred for significant inflammation." },
{ q: "What is iris bombé?", a: "<b>Iris bombé</b> = forward bowing of the entire iris due to <b>360° posterior synechiae (seclusio pupillae)</b>.<br>Aqueous cannot pass from posterior to anterior chamber → builds up behind iris → pushes iris forward.<br>Causes <b>secondary angle-closure glaucoma</b>.<br>Treatment: <b>laser iridotomy</b> (create alternate pathway for aqueous), intensive topical steroids + cycloplegics, IOP-lowering agents." },
{ q: "What is rose bengal staining used for?", a: "<b>Rose bengal</b> stains <b>devitalized and dead epithelial cells</b> and mucus.<br>Clinical uses:<br>• <b>HSV keratitis</b> — stains the edges of dendritic ulcer (dead cells around ulcer margins)<br>• <b>Dry eye (keratoconjunctivitis sicca)</b> — stains interpalpebral conjunctiva/cornea<br>Differs from fluorescein which stains areas of <b>epithelial defect</b> (missing cells)." },
{ q: "What is the most common complication of topical corticosteroid use in the eye?", a: "• <b>Steroid-induced glaucoma</b> (elevated IOP) — occurs in ~30% of the general population, more common in \"steroid responders\"<br>• <b>Posterior subcapsular cataract</b> — with prolonged use<br>• <b>Increased susceptibility to infections</b> (HSV reactivation, fungal keratitis)<br>• Delayed wound healing<br>Always <b>monitor IOP</b> in patients on chronic topical steroids." },
{ q: "What type of cataract is associated with chronic steroid use?", a: "<b>Posterior subcapsular cataract (PSC)</b><br>Develops with <b>chronic systemic or topical corticosteroid</b> use.<br>Symptoms: <b>glare</b> (especially in bright light/driving at night), reading difficulty<br>Location: posterior lens surface, central axis<br>Also associated with: diabetes, uveitis, retinitis pigmentosa, radiation." },
{ q: "What is the most common cause of posterior uveitis worldwide?", a: "<b>Toxoplasma gondii</b> — retinochoroiditis<br>Usually reactivation of <b>congenital infection</b><br>Classic findings: focal necrotizing retinitis adjacent to an old pigmented scar (\"satellite lesion\"), vitritis (headlight in fog)<br>Treatment: pyrimethamine + sulfadiazine + folinic acid<br>Alternative: TMP-SMX" },
{ q: "What is toxocara and how does it affect the eye?", a: "<b>Toxocara canis/cati</b> — roundworm infection acquired from <b>dog/cat feces (contaminated soil)</b><br>Ocular toxocariasis: presents in children as <b>unilateral</b> posterior uveitis<br>Findings: <b>peripheral granuloma</b>, posterior pole granuloma, endophthalmitis<br>Can mimic <b>retinoblastoma</b> (leukocoria)<br>Diagnose with <b>ELISA for Toxocara antibodies</b><br>Treatment: periocular/systemic steroids ± albendazole" },
{ q: "How do you differentiate ocular toxocariasis from retinoblastoma?", a: "Both present with <b>leukocoria</b> (white pupillary reflex) in children.<br><b>Retinoblastoma</b>: <b>calcification</b> on CT/ultrasound, bilateral in 30%, mean age 18 months<br><b>Toxocariasis</b>: <b>no calcification</b>, always unilateral, positive Toxocara ELISA, history of pica/exposure to puppies<br><b>DO NOT biopsy</b> — risk of tumor seeding if retinoblastoma, risk of severe inflammation if toxocara." },
{ q: "What is preseptal vs orbital cellulitis and how are they distinguished?", a: "<b>Preseptal</b>: infection anterior to orbital septum — lid swelling, warmth, no proptosis, <b>normal eye movements/vision</b><br><b>Orbital</b>: infection posterior to septum — proptosis, <b>ophthalmoplegia (painful EOM restriction)</b>, decreased vision, afferent pupillary defect, <b>MEDICAL EMERGENCY</b><br>Orbital cellulitis: CT orbits + IV antibiotics (vancomycin + ceftriaxone or ampicillin-sulbactam), possible surgical drainage<br>Most common cause: <b>ethmoid sinusitis</b> extension" },
{ q: "What is a marginal keratitis and how does it differ from an infectious ulcer?", a: "<b>Marginal keratitis</b>: sterile, peripheral corneal infiltrates due to <b>hypersensitivity reaction to staphylococcal exotoxins</b> from chronic blepharitis.<br>Key differences from infectious ulcer:<br>• Located <b>peripherally</b> (near limbus) with a <b>clear zone</b> between infiltrate and limbus<br>• <b>Less pain, smaller, no hypopyon</b><br>• Culture-negative<br>Treatment: topical steroids (after ruling out infection) + treat underlying blepharitis" },
{ q: "What is interstitial keratitis and what causes it?", a: "<b>Interstitial keratitis (IK)</b> = non-ulcerative inflammation of the corneal <b>stroma</b> without primary epithelial or endothelial involvement.<br>Classic cause: <b>congenital syphilis</b> (bilateral, occurs in late childhood/adolescence) — part of Hutchinson's triad (IK + Hutchinson teeth + CN VIII deafness)<br>Also: TB, Cogan syndrome (IK + vestibulo-auditory dysfunction), HSV, EBV, Lyme<br>Treatment: topical steroids + treat underlying cause" },
{ q: "What is Hutchinson's triad in congenital syphilis?", a: "<b>Hutchinson's triad</b> (late congenital syphilis):<br>1. <b>Interstitial keratitis</b> (bilateral, corneal clouding + vascularization)<br>2. <b>Hutchinson teeth</b> (notched, peg-shaped upper central incisors)<br>3. <b>Eighth nerve deafness</b> (sensorineural hearing loss)<br>Caused by <b>Treponema pallidum</b>, transmitted transplacentally." },
{ q: "What is Cogan syndrome?", a: "<b>Cogan syndrome</b>: rare inflammatory disorder featuring:<br>1. <b>Interstitial keratitis</b> (nonsyphilitic)<br>2. <b>Vestibuloauditory dysfunction</b> (vertigo, tinnitus, sensorineural hearing loss — resembles Ménière disease)<br>May be associated with <b>systemic vasculitis</b> (aortitis, coronary arteritis)<br>Affects young adults<br>Treatment: topical steroids for keratitis, systemic steroids/immunosuppressants for systemic disease" },
{ q: "What is the difference between a corneal ulcer and a corneal infiltrate?", a: "<b>Corneal infiltrate</b>: collection of inflammatory cells in stroma <b>without overlying epithelial defect</b> → does NOT stain with fluorescein (or minimal staining)<br><b>Corneal ulcer</b>: stromal infiltrate <b>with overlying epithelial defect</b> → stains with fluorescein<br>Ulcers are more likely infectious and require culture + aggressive treatment.<br>Sterile infiltrates (contact lens-related) may be observed with close follow-up." },
{ q: "What is Thygeson's superficial punctate keratitis?", a: "<b>Thygeson's SPK</b>: bilateral, chronic, relapsing corneal disease of unknown etiology.<br>Features: multiple, coarse, <b>stellate (star-shaped) epithelial opacities</b> with minimal to no conjunctival injection or anterior chamber reaction<br>Symptoms: foreign body sensation, tearing, photophobia<br>Treatment: <b>soft contact lens</b> (therapeutic) or low-dose topical steroids or cyclosporine<br>Benign course, does not cause permanent vision loss" },
{ q: "What is exposure keratopathy?", a: "<b>Exposure keratopathy</b>: corneal damage from <b>inadequate eyelid closure</b> (incomplete blink or lagophthalmos).<br>Causes: Bell's palsy (CN VII), thyroid eye disease, post-surgical, severe proptosis<br>Inferior cornea dries out → punctate epithelial erosions → ulceration → perforation if untreated<br>Treatment: artificial tears, lubricating ointment, moisture chambers, taping lids shut at night, tarsorrhaphy in severe cases" },
{ q: "What is neurotrophic keratitis?", a: "<b>Neurotrophic keratitis</b>: corneal epithelial breakdown due to <b>loss of corneal innervation</b> (CN V/trigeminal nerve damage).<br>Causes: HSV/HZV keratitis (most common), acoustic neuroma surgery, trigeminal ablation, diabetes<br>Features: <b>absent corneal reflex</b>, persistent epithelial defects, smooth-bordered ulcers, quiet/white eye<br>Treatment: preservative-free artificial tears, bandage contact lens, autologous serum drops, tarsorrhaphy, cenegermin (recombinant nerve growth factor)." },
{ q: "What is recurrent corneal erosion syndrome?", a: "<b>Recurrent corneal erosion</b>: episodic spontaneous breakdown of corneal epithelium, typically upon waking.<br>History: prior corneal abrasion (especially fingernail/paper cut) or <b>epithelial basement membrane dystrophy (EBMD)</b><br>Symptoms: sudden <b>sharp pain upon awakening</b>, tearing, photophobia<br>Mechanism: poor adhesion of regenerated epithelium to basement membrane<br>Treatment: nighttime lubricating ointment, hypertonic saline (5% NaCl), bandage contact lens, anterior stromal micropuncture, phototherapeutic keratectomy (PTK)" },
{ q: "What is a geographic ulcer and when does it occur?", a: "A <b>geographic ulcer</b> is an enlarged, amoeboid-shaped corneal epithelial defect that results from <b>coalescence of HSV dendritic ulcers</b>.<br>Typically occurs when:<br>• HSV keratitis is <b>mistreated with topical steroids without antiviral cover</b><br>• Immunosuppression<br>• Inadequate antiviral therapy<br>It indicates <b>active viral replication</b> with a wider area of epithelial destruction.<br>Treatment: aggressive topical + oral antivirals, debridement." },
{ q: "What is the HEDS (Herpetic Eye Disease Study) key takeaway?", a: "The <b>HEDS</b> demonstrated:<br>• <b>Oral acyclovir 400mg BID</b> as long-term prophylaxis <b>reduces recurrence</b> of HSV keratitis and stromal keratitis by ~50%.<br>• Topical steroids are beneficial for HSV <b>stromal keratitis</b> (immune-mediated) when combined with antiviral cover.<br>• Oral acyclovir does not benefit acute epithelial keratitis but prevents recurrence." },
{ q: "What is HSV stromal keratitis and how is it treated?", a: "<b>HSV stromal keratitis</b> = immune-mediated inflammation of the corneal stroma (not active viral replication).<br>Features: corneal edema/haze, neovascularization, ring infiltrate, lipid keratopathy, intact epithelium<br>Treatment:<br>• <b>Topical steroids (prednisolone)</b> — to reduce immune-mediated damage<br>• <b>WITH concurrent topical/oral antiviral coverage</b> (to prevent viral reactivation from steroids)<br>This is the <b>one scenario where steroids are used in HSV keratitis</b>." },
{ q: "What is the most common cause of infectious corneal ulcer in contact lens wearers?", a: "<b>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</b><br>Risk factors: extended wear, sleeping in lenses, poor hygiene, swimming/showering with lenses<br>Can progress to corneal perforation within 24–48 hours<br>Treatment: topical fluoroquinolone or fortified aminoglycoside, NO patching." },
{ q: "What systemic condition should be screened for in a child with chronic, asymptomatic bilateral anterior uveitis?", a: "<b>Juvenile idiopathic arthritis (JIA)</b><br>Specifically: <b>oligoarticular (pauciarticular), ANA-positive, young girls</b><br>Uveitis is chronic, bilateral, and WHITE (no redness) — often only detected on routine slit-lamp screening<br>Can lead to band keratopathy, cataracts, glaucoma, amblyopia if missed<br>Screen with slit lamp every 3 months" },
{ q: "What is a Mooren ulcer?", a: "<b>Mooren ulcer</b>: idiopathic, progressive <b>peripheral corneal ulceration</b> that is NOT associated with systemic disease (unlike PUK).<br>Features:<br>• Starts peripherally, progresses centrally and circumferentially<br>• Undermined, overhanging central edge<br>• Painful, with significant conjunctival injection<br>• <b>Diagnosis of exclusion</b> (rule out infectious and autoimmune causes)<br>Treatment: topical steroids, conjunctival resection, systemic immunosuppression, limbal stem cell transplant" },
{ q: "What are the components of the aqueous humor outflow pathway?", a: "<b>Aqueous humor</b> (produced by ciliary body) flows:<br>Posterior chamber → through pupil → anterior chamber →<br><b>Conventional (trabecular) pathway (85–90%)</b>: trabecular meshwork → Schlemm's canal → collector channels → episcleral veins<br><b>Uveoscleral (unconventional) pathway (10–15%)</b>: through ciliary muscle interstitial spaces → suprachoroidal space<br>Obstruction → elevated IOP → glaucoma" },
{ q: "What is the significance of preauricular lymphadenopathy in red eye?", a: "<b>Preauricular lymphadenopathy</b> = tender, enlarged lymph node anterior to the ear (parotid region).<br>Indicates <b>viral or chlamydial</b> conjunctivitis:<br>• <b>Adenoviral conjunctivitis</b> (most common cause)<br>• Chlamydial inclusion conjunctivitis<br>• HSV keratitis/conjunctivitis<br>• Parinaud oculoglandular syndrome (cat-scratch disease)<br>Typically <b>absent</b> in bacterial and allergic conjunctivitis." },
{ q: "What is Parinaud oculoglandular syndrome?", a: "<b>Parinaud oculoglandular syndrome</b>: unilateral conjunctivitis with <b>ipsilateral, markedly enlarged preauricular and submandibular lymphadenopathy</b>.<br>Causes:<br>• <b>Cat-scratch disease (Bartonella henselae)</b> — most common<br>• Tularemia (Francisella tularensis)<br>• Sporotrichosis<br>• TB, syphilis<br>Treatment depends on cause; Bartonella: azithromycin" },
{ q: "What is the significance of a hyphema in the context of red eye?", a: "<b>Hyphema</b> = blood in the anterior chamber, usually from <b>trauma</b> (blunt injury tearing iris vessels).<br>Graded by amount: Grade 1 (<1/3 AC) to Grade 4 (total — \"8-ball\" hyphema)<br>Complications: <b>rebleed</b> (days 3–5), elevated IOP, corneal blood staining<br>Treatment: upright position (30°), bed rest, topical steroids + cycloplegic, <b>avoid aspirin/NSAIDs</b>, shield eye<br>Screen for <b>sickle cell</b> trait/disease in African American patients (sickling in AC → elevated IOP)" },
{ q: "What is giant papillary conjunctivitis (GPC)?", a: "<b>GPC</b>: large papillae (>1 mm) on the <b>upper tarsal conjunctiva</b>, resembling cobblestones.<br>Causes: <b>contact lens wear</b> (most common), ocular prosthesis, exposed sutures, scleral buckle<br>Mechanism: <b>mechanical irritation + Type IV hypersensitivity</b> to deposits on lens surface<br>Symptoms: itching, mucoid discharge, lens intolerance<br>Treatment: discontinue/change lens type, mast cell stabilizers, frequent lens replacement, preservative-free care solutions" },
{ q: "What is the difference between a papillary and follicular response?", a: "<b>Papillary</b>: flat-topped elevations with <b>central vascular core</b>, red → <b>allergic and bacterial</b><br><b>Follicular</b>: rounded, avascular, pale/white lymphoid aggregates with vessels <b>around</b> them → <b>viral and chlamydial</b><br><br>Mnemonics:<br>• <b>Papillae</b> = <b>P</b>urulent (bacteria) and allergic<br>• <b>Follicles</b> = viral and chlamydial (<b>F</b>ollicles = like <b>F</b>ollicles of lymphoid tissue)" },
{ q: "What is Lissamine green staining and when is it used?", a: "<b>Lissamine green</b> stains <b>devitalized/dead epithelial cells</b> (similar to rose bengal but less irritating).<br>Used for:<br>• <b>Dry eye disease</b> — stains conjunctival and corneal epithelial damage<br>• Evaluating ocular surface disease<br>• Better tolerated than rose bengal<br>Unlike fluorescein (which stains areas of epithelial loss), lissamine green stains cells that are <b>present but damaged</b>." },
{ q: "What percentage of acute anterior uveitis cases are idiopathic?", a: "Approximately <b>50%</b> of acute anterior uveitis cases are <b>idiopathic</b> (no identifiable systemic cause).<br>This is why a first episode in a young, healthy patient may require <b>limited workup</b>.<br>Full systemic workup is reserved for: <b>recurrent, bilateral, granulomatous, posterior, pediatric, or refractory</b> uveitis." },
{ q: "What is the triad of findings in herpes zoster ophthalmicus?", a: "1. <b>Vesicular dermatomal rash</b> in V1 distribution (does NOT cross midline)<br>2. <b>Hutchinson sign</b> (nasociliary involvement → tip of nose lesion)<br>3. <b>Ocular complications</b>: keratitis (pseudodendrites), uveitis, elevated IOP, cranial nerve palsies (III, IV, VI), postherpetic neuralgia<br>Treatment: oral antivirals (valacyclovir) within 72h + ophthalmology referral" },
{ q: "What is an afferent pupillary defect (APD/RAPD) and is it seen in red eye conditions?", a: "<b>Relative afferent pupillary defect (RAPD/Marcus Gunn pupil)</b>: detected by swinging flashlight test — affected pupil <b>dilates</b> when light is swung to it from the normal eye.<br>Indicates optic nerve or extensive retinal disease on the affected side.<br>In red eye: generally <b>absent</b> in conjunctivitis, keratitis, anterior uveitis<br><b>Present</b> in: severe optic neuritis, central retinal artery/vein occlusion, orbital cellulitis compressing optic nerve, severe posterior uveitis" },
{ q: "What is the otitis-conjunctivitis syndrome?", a: "<b>Otitis-conjunctivitis syndrome</b>: concurrent <b>acute otitis media + purulent conjunctivitis</b>, typically in children.<br>Most common organism: <b>nontypeable Haemophilus influenzae</b><br>Treatment: <b>oral amoxicillin-clavulanate</b> (covers H. influenzae including β-lactamase producers) + topical antibiotic drops<br>A systemic antibiotic is needed because of the concurrent AOM." },
{ q: "What stains are used to identify specific organisms in corneal scrapings?", a: "<b>Gram stain</b>: bacteria (Gram-positive vs Gram-negative)<br><b>Giemsa stain</b>: Chlamydia (basophilic intracytoplasmic inclusions), Acanthamoeba<br><b>KOH preparation</b>: fungal hyphae<br><b>Calcofluor white</b>: fungi (fluorescence under UV) and Acanthamoeba cysts<br><b>Acid-fast stain (Ziehl-Neelsen)</b>: Mycobacteria, Nocardia<br><b>Acridine orange</b>: bacteria, fungi, Acanthamoeba" },
{ q: "What is the significance of nummular/coin-shaped subepithelial infiltrates?", a: "<b>Subepithelial infiltrates (SEIs)</b>: round, coin-shaped, whitish deposits beneath the corneal epithelium.<br>Classic association: <b>adenoviral keratoconjunctivitis (EKC)</b><br>Represent <b>immune complexes</b> (antigen-antibody deposits) — appear 1–3 weeks after acute infection<br>Can persist for <b>months to years</b><br>Cause: glare, halos, mild decreased vision<br>Treatment: topical steroids if visually significant (but may recur on discontinuation)" },
{ q: "What is the mainstay treatment for severe/sight-threatening scleritis?", a: "<b>Systemic NSAIDs</b> (indomethacin, ibuprofen) — first-line for non-necrotizing scleritis<br><b>Systemic corticosteroids</b> — if NSAIDs fail<br><b>Immunosuppressants</b> — for necrotizing scleritis or steroid-dependent cases:<br>• Methotrexate, mycophenolate mofetil<br>• Cyclophosphamide (for GPA-associated necrotizing scleritis)<br>• <b>Rituximab</b> (refractory cases)<br>Topical therapy alone is <b>insufficient</b> for scleritis." },
{ q: "How does contact lens overwear cause corneal problems?", a: "Prolonged contact lens wear leads to:<br>• <b>Corneal hypoxia</b> → epithelial edema, neovascularization, microcystic changes<br>• <b>Mechanical abrasion</b> → erosions, giant papillary conjunctivitis<br>• <b>Biofilm formation</b> → increases risk of <b>Pseudomonas and Acanthamoeba</b> keratitis<br>• <b>Altered corneal flora</b> → susceptibility to infection<br>Prevention: proper hygiene, avoid overnight wear, replace as recommended, never use tap water" },
{ q: "What is the most important initial step when evaluating a patient with a red eye?", a: "1. <b>Check visual acuity</b> — the vital sign of ophthalmology<br>2. Assess pain severity and character<br>3. Determine pattern of injection (diffuse vs ciliary flush)<br>4. Examine pupils (size, reactivity, APD)<br>5. Evaluate discharge (type and amount)<br>6. Slit-lamp examination (AC cells/flare, corneal staining)<br>7. Measure IOP (tonometry)<br>Decreased visual acuity = urgent referral to ophthalmology" },
{ q: "When should a patient with a red eye be referred urgently to ophthalmology?", a: "Urgent referral indicated for:<br>• <b>Decreased visual acuity</b><br>• <b>Severe pain</b><br>• <b>Corneal opacity/ulcer</b><br>• <b>Hypopyon</b><br>• <b>Pupil abnormality</b> (fixed, irregular, APD)<br>• <b>Suspected penetrating injury</b> (positive Seidel test)<br>• <b>Suspected acute glaucoma</b><br>• <b>Cells and flare</b> on slit lamp (uveitis)<br>• <b>Hyperacute purulent conjunctivitis</b> (gonococcal)<br>• <b>Herpetic eye disease</b>" },
{ q: "What is disciform keratitis?", a: "<b>Disciform (endotheliitis) keratitis</b>: a disc-shaped area of <b>corneal edema</b> with underlying <b>keratic precipitates</b> on the endothelium.<br>Represents <b>immune-mediated endothelial dysfunction</b>.<br>Most commonly caused by <b>HSV</b> (also HZV, CMV).<br>Treatment: <b>topical steroids + antiviral cover</b><br>Untreated → permanent endothelial damage → corneal decompensation." },
{ q: "What ocular finding is strongly associated with Lyme disease?", a: "<b>Lyme disease</b> (Borrelia burgdorferi) ocular manifestations:<br>• <b>Cranial nerve palsies</b> (CN VII bilateral = Bell's palsy; CN III, IV, VI)<br>• <b>Follicular conjunctivitis</b> (early disease)<br>• <b>Intermediate uveitis</b> (vitritis)<br>• Keratitis (stromal/interstitial)<br>• Optic neuritis, papilledema<br>Treatment: doxycycline (early), IV ceftriaxone (neuro-ophthalmic involvement)" },
{ q: "A 25-year-old male with painful oral ulcers and genital ulcers develops a red eye with hypopyon. Diagnosis?", a: "<b>Behçet disease</b><br>Classic triad: recurrent oral aphthous ulcers + genital ulcers + uveitis<br>Hypopyon uveitis (shifting, sterile) is characteristic<br>Also: retinal vasculitis, pathergy (skin hyperreactivity)<br>HLA-B51 associated<br>Treatment: colchicine, azathioprine, cyclosporine, anti-TNF agents<br>Common along the Silk Road" },
{ q: "What is the Munson sign?", a: "<b>Munson sign</b>: a V-shaped indentation of the lower eyelid when the patient looks downward, caused by <b>keratoconus</b> — progressive corneal thinning and ectasia (cone-shaped cornea).<br>Other signs of keratoconus: <b>Vogt striae</b> (fine vertical lines in deep stroma), <b>Fleischer ring</b> (iron deposition at base of cone), <b>scissoring reflex</b> on retinoscopy, <b>hydrops</b> (acute corneal edema from Descemet's rupture)." },
{ q: "What is the difference between granulomatous and non-granulomatous uveitis?", a: "<b>Non-granulomatous</b>:<br>• Fine, dust-like KPs<br>• No iris nodules<br>• Acute onset, unilateral<br>• Causes: HLA-B27, idiopathic, Fuchs<br><br><b>Granulomatous</b>:<br>• <b>Mutton-fat KPs</b> (large, greasy)<br>• <b>Iris nodules</b> (Koeppe, Busacca)<br>• Often chronic, bilateral<br>• Causes: <b>Sarcoidosis, TB, syphilis, VKH, sympathetic ophthalmia</b><br>Mnemonic for granulomatous: <b>S</b>arcoid, <b>S</b>yphilis, <b>T</b>B, <b>V</b>KH, <b>S</b>ympathetic ophthalmia" },
];
